<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taifa of Málaga</h1><p class="st-meta">1026 CE – 1057 CE (approx.) · capital Málaga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Hammudid taifa of Málaga, ruled by a Berberised Arab dynasty that claimed the caliphate of al-Andalus after the Umayyads fell.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammudid-dynasty">Hammudids</a>, descendants of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisids</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a>, had briefly held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> from 1016. Driven from the capital, they made Málaga their base around 1026, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-ali">Yahya ibn Ali al-Mutali</a> established his court there while still claiming the title of caliph.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammudid-dynasty">Hammudids</a> led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> party in the fitna and were recognised as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> by several Berber <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronda">Ronda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmona">Carmona</a>. Their state, spanning Málaga and at times <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a>, was weakened by succession quarrels, and real power often lay with Berber commanders and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zirid-emirate">Zirids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1057 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badis-ibn-habus">Badis</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taifa-of-granada">Taifa of Granada</a> annexed Málaga, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammudid-dynasty">Hammudid</a> line in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. The city was later briefly independent again as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-taifa-of-malaga">Second Taifa of Málaga</a> under a Zirid prince.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taifa-of-malaga.jpg" alt="Taifa of Málaga" width="320" height="249"><figcaption>Image: Tyk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_map_Taifa_of_M%C3%A1laga.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1026 CE – 1057 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Málaga</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yahya ibn Ali al-Mutali, Idris I, Idris II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hammudid Málaga</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1042&amp;focus=taifa-of-malaga" aria-label="Taifa of Málaga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4569 854 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taifa of Málaga lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4889 1110L4863 1130M4889 1110L4866 1138M4889 1110L4881 1071M4889 1110L4910 1095"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4863 1130h0M4866 1138h0M4881 1071h0M4910 1095h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4889 1110h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4889 1110h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Málaga. <a href="/atlas?year=1042&amp;focus=taifa-of-malaga">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
